\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Oscilloscope capture of the I2C bus showing synchronous two-wire serial communication between the RP2040 microcontroller and the LIS3DH accelerometer at a 400 kHz bus clock frequency. Channel 2 (blue) displays the clock line ($\text  {SCL}$, GPIO 3) providing regular timing pulses, while Channel 1 (orange) displays the data line ($\text  {SDA}$, GPIO 2). The visible bursts correspond to data transactions, such as addressing the accelerometer (\texttt  {0x18}) and reading/writing internal configuration and data registers (e.g., polling \texttt  {WHO\_AM\_I} or pulling axis measurements via \texttt  {accel.update()}).}}{1}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:i2c_wave}{{1}{1}{Oscilloscope capture of the I2C bus showing synchronous two-wire serial communication between the RP2040 microcontroller and the LIS3DH accelerometer at a 400 kHz bus clock frequency. Channel 2 (blue) displays the clock line ($\text {SCL}$, GPIO 3) providing regular timing pulses, while Channel 1 (orange) displays the data line ($\text {SDA}$, GPIO 2). The visible bursts correspond to data transactions, such as addressing the accelerometer (\texttt {0x18}) and reading/writing internal configuration and data registers (e.g., polling \texttt {WHO\_AM\_I} or pulling axis measurements via \texttt {accel.update()})}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Oscilloscope capture of the NeoMatrix output line implementing a strict single-wire timing protocol used to drive WS2812 RGB LEDs. Generated via the RP2040's Programmable I/O (PIO) state machine on GPIO 7 at 800 kHz, the waveform uses asymmetric pulse-width modulation where each high-frequency pulse encodes binary data based on precise duration timings ($T_1$, $T_2$, $T_3$). The repeated packet trains represent 24-bit GRB color frames shifted out sequentially to update all 64 pixels in the matrix buffer.}}{2}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:neomatrix_wave}{{2}{2}{Oscilloscope capture of the NeoMatrix output line implementing a strict single-wire timing protocol used to drive WS2812 RGB LEDs. Generated via the RP2040's Programmable I/O (PIO) state machine on GPIO 7 at 800 kHz, the waveform uses asymmetric pulse-width modulation where each high-frequency pulse encodes binary data based on precise duration timings ($T_1$, $T_2$, $T_3$). The repeated packet trains represent 24-bit GRB color frames shifted out sequentially to update all 64 pixels in the matrix buffer}{figure.2}{}}
